        "eyebrow": "2026 — Stoneware",
        "heading": "Assembly",
        "body": "<p>Nobody arrives finished. You are assembled — one experience at a time, from childhood forward, each one joined to the last until the shape of you is something no single part accounts for. Assembly is built the same way.</p><p>Each form is pressed, hollowed and left to stiffen, then scored, slipped and joined to the next — a grid built up row by row until it stands on its own. The face arrives later. Nothing about a stack of spheres suggests one, until the features go on and then it is impossible to see anything else. That turn is the piece.</p><p>Each form carries a number, and a number can be made more than once — never in the same glaze combination. Assembly 01 exists twice: in a crystal glaze, a gloss base carrying chips of glass frit that bloom open in the fire and run down the form, and in white underglaze flicked over a cobalt glaze. Assembly 02 is the second form — taller, with brows and filled lenses — in a yellow glaze, its features in purple underglaze under clear.</p>",
        "intro_ground": "chalk",
        "notice_label": "On view",
        "notice": "<p><em>The Presidents’ Show</em> — American Ceramic Society, Southern California Chapter. Beato Gallery, Beatrice Wood Center for the Arts, Ojai. 26 September – 8 November 2026.</p>",
        "notice_ground": "chalk",
        "gallery_ratio": "1 / 1",
        "gallery_ground": "chalk",
        "statement_eyebrow": "The idea",
        "statement": "No one is cast in one piece. We are assembled.",
        "statement_support": "<p>Part joined to part, one at a time, until the whole reads as a face and no single part accounts for it. That is what this series is.</p>",
